<Guide title="How to find crisis support while travelling abroad" description="A practical guide to finding local emergency numbers and crisis-support services while travelling, without assuming your home-country contacts work abroad." researched="15 August 2026">
  <p>A familiar emergency number or crisis line may not work after you cross a border. Short codes, toll-free numbers, eligibility rules, languages, and service areas can all change by country.</p>
  <p>If you need help while travelling, begin with your current location—not your nationality, home telephone number, or browser language.</p>
  <blockquote><p>If someone is in immediate danger, seriously injured, or needs an urgent public emergency response, use the emergency number published by the responsible authority for the country you are currently in.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>1. Confirm the country you are physically in</h2>
  <p>Start with the country or territory where help is needed. This matters even if your telephone still has a home-country SIM or number.</p>
  <p>Use the <a href="/countries">country directory</a> or <a href="/map">interactive map</a> to open the relevant local page. Do not assume that a number used at home will route correctly abroad, and do not infer a number from a neighbouring country.</p>
  <p>Border regions and overseas territories can have different systems from the country you recently left. When the jurisdiction is unclear, check the responsible government or local emergency-service authority for the exact location. Information from an airport, transport operator, accommodation provider, or consular service can be a lead, but verify it against the responsible local authority where circumstances allow.</p>

  <h2>2. Separate an emergency response from crisis support</h2>
  <p>An emergency number is intended for situations requiring an urgent public response, such as an ambulance, fire service, or police. A crisis hotline may provide emotional support, specialist information, or help related to a particular situation, but it may not dispatch emergency services.</p>
  <p>If there is immediate danger, finding a support line should not delay contacting the responsible local emergency service.</p>
  <p>The European Commission states that 112 is available throughout the European Union and can connect callers to ambulance, fire, or police services. It also states that 112 operates alongside national emergency numbers in many EU countries. That guidance has a defined EU scope; it should not be turned into a worldwide assumption.</p>

  <h2>3. Look for the right support category</h2>
  <p>If the situation does not require an immediate public emergency response—or if additional support is needed—browse the relevant category. Examples include:</p>
  <ul><li>suicide or emotional crisis;</li><li>mental health;</li><li>domestic or sexual violence;</li><li>child protection;</li><li>substance use;</li><li>trafficking;</li><li>refugee or migrant support; and</li><li>general emotional support.</li></ul>
  <p>Read the individual listing before contacting it. A category describes the subject of a service; it does not establish eligibility, quality, current availability, or suitability for a particular person.</p>

  <h2>4. Check the service area and audience</h2>
  <p>A national number may work throughout one country. Another service may cover only a city, state, province, or defined region. Some lines serve particular age groups, professions, languages, or communities.</p>
  <p>Look for explicit information about:</p>
  <ul><li>country and local service area;</li><li>intended audience;</li><li>opening hours;</li><li>supported languages;</li><li>telephone, SMS, chat, or email channels; and</li><li>whether a published number is local, toll-free, or a short code.</li></ul>
  <p>If the listing does not establish one of these facts, treat it as unknown. Do not assume that a service is international because its website can be opened internationally.</p>

  <h2>5. Choose a channel that can work abroad</h2>
  <p>Telephone access can change while roaming. A toll-free number or short code may require a local SIM or network. International dialling may require a country prefix, but adding a prefix to a short code does not necessarily create a working international number.</p>
  <p>If the provider publishes an online-chat channel, it may be reachable over Wi-Fi or mobile data. That does not guarantee that the provider accepts contacts from outside its service area or is available at that moment.</p>
  <p>SMS access may also depend on your carrier, roaming plan, number type, and the provider’s system. Use only the channel explicitly published by the provider; do not send a text message to a voice number unless the service says it accepts texts.</p>

  <h2>6. Verify important details at the source</h2>
  <p>Directory records include source and verification-status information where available. These labels describe what evidence was checked for the directory—not a live test that the service answered.</p>
  <p>Before relying on details such as hours, language, eligibility, or geographic scope, check the provider’s current page or the responsible authority’s guidance when possible. A recent source date does not prove that a service is open now, and an older source does not by itself prove that a number has stopped working.</p>
  <p>If material sources conflict, treat the information as unresolved. Do not combine or guess between claims; seek current guidance for the exact location from the responsible emergency authority or provider. If urgent help is needed, follow the responsible local emergency authority’s instructions.</p>

  <h2>7. Use local people carefully</h2>
  <p>Accommodation staff, transport personnel, a tour operator, school, employer, embassy, consulate, hospital, or local authority may help identify the responsible service. Treat any number they provide as a lead to verify where circumstances allow.</p>
  <p>Consular services can explain what assistance they offer citizens abroad, but they are not a replacement for local emergency responders.</p>

  <h2>Before you travel</h2>
  <p>A small amount of preparation can reduce confusion later:</p>
  <ul><li>save the official emergency guidance for your destination;</li><li>note how to access your travel insurer or assistance provider;</li><li>check whether your mobile plan supports roaming calls and SMS;</li><li>retain important medical or accessibility information in a form you can share safely; and</li><li>bookmark the relevant <a href="/countries">World Hotlines country page</a>.</li></ul>
  <p>Do not store sensitive personal information in a public note or shared device merely for convenience.</p>

  <h2>If a listing appears wrong</h2>
  <p>Report disconnected numbers, changed hours, incorrect service areas, closures, or misleading channel information through the correction link on the relevant service card. Include a public provider or authority source where possible, but do not include private crisis details.</p>

